<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as emirs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An emir is an Arabic title meaning commander or prince, used for military leaders, governors and rulers in Islamic states. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> emirs were the senior military officers, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> powerful emirs dominated late politics, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> emirs governed the constituent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emir.jpg" alt="Emir" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja_Shah_Durrani_and_Men_in_his_decorated_palace_Wellcome_V0050531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> comes from the Arabic root a-m-r, meaning command, and meant lord or commander-in-chief. It came to be used as a title of leaders, governors and rulers of smaller states. The word entered English in 1593, from the French émir. It has been used in West Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and the feminine form is emira. From the start it was a military title, and in the ninth century it was also used for the ruler of a state, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> first used the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>, Commander of the Faithful, to stress their leadership over the Islamic empire, especially its militia, and Shia Muslims still give it to the Caliph Ali. Various Muslim rulers later took it, sultans and emirs among them. The Abbasid caliph al-Radi created the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-Umara, Amir of the Amirs, for Ibn Raik, and the title was used in various Islamic monarchies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> the ruling emir formally used the style al-Amir al-Hakim, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Christians both held the title. The theocratic and hereditary leader of the Yazidi community, the Mîr of the Sheikhan principality, is known as an emir or prince.</p><p>In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> the traditional rulers are called emirs, while the titular sovereign of the former Sokoto empire is styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-of-sokoto">Sultan of Sokoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-Muminin. The title of emir has gradually given way to king among hereditary rulers who wish to stress their secular authority under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>'s monarch changed his title from emir to king in 2002, and the United Arab Emirates use sheikh rather than emir for their rulers of the individual emirates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In certain decimally organised Muslim armies amir was an officer rank. In Mughal India the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> commanded 1,000 horsemen, divided into ten units each under a sipah salar, and ten of them stood under one malik. In the imperial army of Qajar Persia there were ranks such as Amir Panj, commander of 5,000, and Amir-i-Tuman, commander of 10,000. Medieval Muslim states had posts such as amir al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>, commander of the pilgrim caravan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and amir al-ʿarab, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes.</p><p>In the former Kingdom of Afghanistan Amir-i-Kabir meant great prince or great commander, and in imperial Persia Amir-i-Iel designated the head of a tribe. The son of a prince was an amirzade, from which the Persian princely title mirza comes. The Fatimid navy had a post called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-Baḥr, commander of the sea, often mistaken for the origin of the English admiral. The titles of admiral actually derive from Medieval Latin forms of emir itself, first used for the amirs al-umara of Norman Sicily.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Aghlabid <b>emirs</b> were Mu'tazilites who held that the Qur'an was created, a doctrine the Maliki majority of Ifriqiya rejected; the scholar Sahsun was persecuted under Muhammad I for refusing it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>For three decades he extended his authority over neighbouring <b>emirs</b> and fought the Qara Qoyunlu, until he was killed in battle against them near Erzurum in 1435.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1275 the <b>emirs</b> took Tarsus, burnt the palace, killed 15,000 civilians and took 10,000 to Egypt; almost all of Ayas's people perished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In Baghdad the Abbasid caliph remained the formal source of legitimate authority for Sunni Muslims, invested the Buyid <b>emirs</b>, and was named in the Friday prayer, but he lost all real power: the emir controlled the army, the treasury and appointments, and the caliph lived on a pension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebellions in the north around Bukhara led Ahmad Shah and the Uzbek <b>Emir</b> of Bukhara to agree that the Amu Darya would divide their lands, and he may have sought a similar sphere of influence with the Qing, as he had with the Ottomans, who divided Iran with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy was based on irrigated agriculture in the river valleys of the south, grain, olives and vines, livestock and textile production, and the <b>emirs</b> struck silver dirhams at Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
